# Pi Guidelines

Applies in addition to `~/AGENTS.md`.

## Delegation

{{ if eq .machine_type "work" -}}
Delegation to pi-subagents is authorized without asking when it adds distinct value:

- unfamiliar code spans several components (`scout`); routine file lookup stays
  in the parent
- external documentation or substantial investigation is needed (`researcher`)
- substantial implementation needs one focused review (`reviewer`); small
  mechanical changes use parent verification
- a concrete architectural uncertainty, consequential decision, or repeated
  failure warrants an independent second opinion (`oracle`, read-only)

These rules override automatic delegation and review fanout in skills. Avoid
routine review fanout; use multiple reviewers only for distinct, justified
questions. Resume an existing child for follow-ups instead of repeating its
investigation. Give fresh children the relevant paths, requirements, and
findings; fork context only when the conversation itself is needed. Return
focused findings to the parent rather than copying full transcripts.
Do not automatically escalate models or fall back to another provider.
{{ else -}}
Delegation to pi-subagents is authorized without asking for:

- codebase recon before implementing (`scout`)
- external docs/API research (`researcher`)
- post-implementation review fanout (`reviewer`)
- second opinion before risky or irreversible changes (`oracle`, read-only)

{{ end -}}

Keep implementation in the parent session unless the task is multi-seam or
unless I pick subagent-driven execution at a superpowers plan handoff; that
authorizes its per-task implementer and reviewer subagents.
One writer per worktree. Launch async and continue independent work; yield when
the next step depends on a child result.

The no-overwrite rule in `~/AGENTS.md` applies to subagent writer lanes too.

On Cursor models (`cursor/*`), always delegate through the `subagent` tool
(exposed to Cursor as `pi__subagent`). Never use Cursor's built-in Task or
subagent tool: it inherits the parent model and thinking level and ignores the
configured agent overrides.

See the bundled pi-subagents skill for orchestration mechanics.

## Asking the user

Use Plannotator Inbox for every question, clarification and decision, including
brainstorming, requirements gathering and questions raised during an active
chat. These rules override question routing and cadence in skills and the
Plannotator-managed note below, including its chat exceptions.

The coding-task review handoff below is the explicit exception: mirror its
invitation in Inbox and the session as one review request.

- Use the `plannotator_inbox` tool (`pi__plannotator_inbox` on Cursor). Consult
  the `plannotator` skill for supported actions and the CLI fallback.
- Ask only what the conversation and available sources cannot answer. Put
  each decision in its own `:::question` block with enough context to answer
  without reading the chat. Group independent questions in one message; ask
  dependent follow-ups after the earlier answers arrive.
- Keep one thread per work stream. Read the thread before continuing it if
  its contents are not in the current context. Keep answers and follow-ups in
  that thread; do not duplicate questions in chat or another question tool.
- Put diagrams and screenshots in the message and attach the relevant files.
  Use Inbox instead of an interview form or a separate brainstorming visual
  companion.
- After sending, continue independent work. Leave dependent work pending and
  end the turn when nothing independent remains. A missing reply is not
  approval. Let replies return through Pi's integration; use `read_thread`
  when automatic delivery is unavailable.
- Hand over finished long work in the same thread with a short summary and
  the files to read. Chat may report status and link to the Inbox thread,
  but must not become a second question or approval channel.
- If Inbox is unavailable, report the connection problem and keep dependent
  work pending. Do not fall back to terminal questions, interview forms or
  chat questions.

